<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frédéric Chopin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1810">1810</a>–<a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Frederic Chopin, Fryderyk Chopin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frédéric Chopin was a Polish composer and virtuoso pianist of the early Romantic period who wrote primarily for solo piano. Raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> less than a month before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/november-uprising">November Uprising</a> of 1830 broke out and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1831, never to return. There he gave only some thirty public performances, preferring the salon, and lived by selling his compositions and teaching the piano. His mazurkas and polonaises have been credited with bringing a new sense of nationalism to music, and he created the ballade as an instrumental genre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederic-chopin.jpg" alt="Frédéric Chopin" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis-Auguste Bisson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frederic_Chopin_photo.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frédéric François Chopin was born in Żelazowa Wola, 46 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, in the Duchy of Warsaw. The baptismal record gives his birthday as 22 February 1810, but the composer and his family used 1 March, now generally accepted. His father, Nicolas Chopin, was a Frenchman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> who had emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1787 and insisted on Polish at home; his mother was Justyna Krzyżanowska. In October 1810 the family moved to Warsaw, where his father taught French at the Warsaw Lyceum. His first professional tutor, from 1816 to 1821, was the pianist Wojciech Żywny. A child prodigy, Chopin gave public concerts by the age of seven.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Chopin wrote his early works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, among them six works for piano and orchestra, including two piano concertos. He left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> at 20, less than a month before the November 1830 Uprising, and at 21 settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He gave only about thirty public performances, preferring the salon, and lived by selling compositions and giving piano lessons, for which he was in high demand. He befriended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a> and was admired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-schumann">Robert Schumann</a>. After a failed engagement to Maria Wodzińska in 1836 to 1837 he kept up an often troubled relationship with the writer George Sand. Their brief and unhappy visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Mallorca</a> in 1838 to 1839 proved one of his most productive periods of composition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>More than 230 of Chopin's works survive, all involving the piano. His influences included Bach and Mozart, Polish folk music and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> salons. He took the nocturne, invented by John Field, to a deeper level of sophistication, was the first to write ballades and scherzi as individual concert pieces, and established the free-standing prelude with his Op. 28, published in 1839. His mazurkas were written for the concert hall rather than the dance hall, and his waltzes for the salon.</p><p>In his last years Jane Stirling supported him financially. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 17 October 1849 at the age of 39. Debussy dedicated his 1915 piano Études to Chopin's memory, and the Fryderyk Chopin Institute, created by the Polish parliament, hosts the International Chopin Piano Competition.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frédéric Chopin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Franz Schubert set more than six hundred poems to music, and the Polish pianist <b>Frédéric Chopin</b>, who settled in Paris in 1831 as the November Uprising in Poland was crushed, turned the memory of his country into mazurkas and polonaises.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · People</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Żelazowa Wola, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.25,20.32&amp;year=1835" aria-label="Żelazowa Wola on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5007 360 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frédéric Chopin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5457 630h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5457 630h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.25,20.32&amp;year=1835">What was here in 1835? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
